# 6. MVP USER FLOW

## Screen 1 — Home

Show:

- "New Scan"
- "Scan History"
- "Light Test"
- "CV Debug Lab"
- Settings

Main CTA:
**New Scan**


## Screen 2 — Select Vehicle Panel

Initial supported panel types:

- Front Left Door
- Front Right Door
- Rear Left Door
- Rear Right Door
- Hood
- Roof
- Front Fender
- Rear Quarter
- Other

Do NOT require vehicle VIN, make, model, customer, or CRM data in MVP.

Optional free-text label.


## Screen 3 — Scan Setup

Allow selection:

### Pattern mode
- Auto
- Vertical stripes
- Horizontal stripes
- Fine stripes
- Wide stripes
- Moving stripes

### Brightness
- slider from 30% to 100%

### Stripe width
- configurable

### Stripe spacing
- configurable

### Color
MVP:
- white on black
- black on white

Later:
- warm white
- RGB
- alternate colors

Default:
- Auto


## Screen 4 — Scan

Critical requirement:

The phone screen must display the structured-light pattern while the front-facing camera simultaneously records frames.

The camera preview does NOT need to occupy the whole screen.

Possible layout:

- nearly full-screen light pattern
- small translucent scan status UI
- scan timer
- movement indicator
- stop button

The structured-light pattern is the priority.

Avoid large UI areas that reduce usable light-emitting surface.

During scan show minimal status:

- READY
- MOVE SLOWLY
- TOO FAST
- LOW SIGNAL
- GOOD SIGNAL
- SCANNING

Target initial scan duration:
5–15 seconds.

Store camera frames or video locally for offline processing.


---

# 7. STRUCTURED LIGHT ENGINE

Create a reusable pattern renderer.

Model:

enum LightPatternType {
  verticalStripes,
  horizontalStripes,
  fineVertical,
  wideVertical,
  movingVertical,
  movingHorizontal,
  checkerboard,
  phaseShiftedVertical,
}

Each pattern must have:

- id
- type
- stripeWidth
- stripeSpacing
- brightness
- foregroundColor
- backgroundColor
- movementSpeed
- phase
- orientation
- timestamp

The exact pattern displayed at every captured frame must be known.

This is important.

For animated patterns, store:

cameraFrameTimestamp
patternTimestamp
patternPhase

so the processing pipeline knows what light configuration existed during the frame.


---

# 8. AUTO SCAN MODE

The MVP should support an experimental automatic sequence.

Example:

Phase 1:
- wide vertical stripes
- 1 second

Phase 2:
- medium vertical stripes
- 1 second

Phase 3:
- fine vertical stripes
- 1 second

Phase 4:
- vertically shifted stripes
- 1 second

Optional:
Phase 5:
- horizontal stripes

Purpose:
Use different spatial frequencies and phases to better identify surface deformation.

Make the sequence configurable.

Do not hardcode the algorithm deeply into UI code.


---

# 9. CAMERA CAPTURE REQUIREMENTS

Use front-facing camera.

Target:
- 1080p when available
- minimum acceptable MVP: 720p
- target 30 FPS

If device overheats or processing is too slow:
- record at 30 FPS
- process sampled frames at 5–15 FPS

Camera controls if supported:
- disable beauty processing
- disable portrait mode
- disable face filters
- lock exposure
- lock focus
- lock white balance

The app must record metadata:
- resolution
- FPS
- camera model if available
- exposure information if available
- device model
- OS version


---

# 10. CV PIPELINE — VERSION 0

The first CV implementation must be explicit and debuggable.

Pipeline:

1. Camera frame
2. Crop optional ROI
3. Convert to grayscale
4. Normalize contrast
5. Denoise
6. Detect bright/dark stripes
7. Extract stripe centerlines
8. Estimate global surface trend
9. Detect local deviations
10. Aggregate deviations across neighboring lines
11. Track deviations across frames
12. Create dent candidates
13. Score confidence
14. Merge overlapping candidates
15. Output dent map


---

# 11. STRIPE DETECTION

Goal:
Identify reflected structured-light stripes on the painted panel.

Possible methods:

Version A:
- grayscale
- adaptive threshold
- morphological operations
- contour detection
- skeletonization
- polyline fitting

Version B:
- edge detection
- Hough transform
- curve fitting

Version C:
- sample intensity perpendicular to expected stripe direction
- detect local maxima/minima
- track stripe centers frame-to-frame

Prefer Version C if reflection quality is sufficient.

Do not assume stripes remain straight.

Car body panels naturally curve.

The algorithm must estimate the NORMAL smooth curvature of the panel.

Dent detection should look for LOCAL distortions relative to the smooth baseline.


---

# 12. DENT CANDIDATE DETECTION

A dent candidate is a localized area where multiple neighboring stripe centerlines show correlated deformation.

Important:
One distorted pixel or one distorted line is NOT enough.

Candidate conditions may include:

- distortion exists across several adjacent stripes
- distortion is spatially localized
- distortion persists across multiple frames
- distortion moves consistently with the surface during camera motion
- anomaly magnitude exceeds local baseline noise

Create:

class DentCandidate {
  id
  normalizedX
  normalizedY
  radiusEstimate
  severity
  confidence
  firstFrame
  lastFrame
  supportingFrames
}

Severity MVP:
- small
- medium
- large

Confidence:
0.0–1.0

Do not call radiusEstimate a true physical millimeter size yet.


---

# 13. FRAME STABILIZATION

The phone will move by hand.

The pipeline must compensate for motion.

Initial approach:
- optical flow
- feature matching
- homography where appropriate

Potential OpenCV techniques:
- ORB
- Lucas-Kanade optical flow
- RANSAC
- perspective transform

The goal is not to reconstruct the full 3D panel.

The goal is to align observations well enough to avoid counting the same dent repeatedly.


---

# 14. TEMPORAL AGGREGATION

Very important.

A dent detected in one frame must be tracked across neighboring frames.

Implement:
- spatial proximity matching
- confidence accumulation
- duplicate suppression

A candidate becomes "confirmed" only when observed in multiple frames.

Example:
- 1 frame = weak candidate
- 3+ consistent frames = medium confidence
- 5+ consistent frames = high confidence

Numbers must be configurable.

Do not hardcode thresholds everywhere.
Put them in CV configuration.


---

# 15. RESULT VIEW

After processing, show:

- panel name
- original/reference image
- overlay with detected candidates
- total candidate count
- confidence per dent
- severity class
- scan quality score

Example:

Front Left Door

Detected: 7 probable dents

Small: 4
Medium: 2
Large: 1

Overlay:
- circles around candidate areas

Color coding:
- low confidence
- medium confidence
- high confidence

Do not imply perfect certainty.

Use wording:
- "Probable dent"
- "Candidate"
- "Detected anomaly"

until validation proves accuracy.


---

# 16. SCAN QUALITY SCORE

Create a simple scan quality metric based on:

- stripe visibility
- brightness
- motion speed
- motion blur
- camera stability
- number of usable frames
- detected surface area

Output:
0–100

Statuses:
- Poor
- Acceptable
- Good
- Excellent

If quality is poor:
recommend rescanning.


---

# 17. LIGHT TEST MODE

This is a required MVP development feature.

Purpose:
Test the optical concept without running the full dent detection pipeline.

Screen should allow:

- live pattern selection
- brightness control
- stripe width
- stripe spacing
- animation speed
- orientation

At the same time:
- front camera records
- optionally save test clip

This tool is essential for physical testing with real car panels.


---

# 18. CV DEBUG LAB

This is mandatory for MVP.

Do not hide the CV pipeline.

Allow developer/tester to inspect:

- raw camera frame
- grayscale
- contrast-normalized frame
- edge map
- detected stripes
- fitted stripe centerlines
- baseline curve
- anomaly heatmap
- final dent candidates

Allow:
- pause frame
- move frame-by-frame
- adjust thresholds
- rerun processing

Make CV parameters editable through a debug settings screen.

This will dramatically accelerate development.


---

# 19. DEBUG CONFIGURATION

Create centralized configuration.

Example:

class CvConfig {
  blurRadius
  contrastLimit
  thresholdBlockSize
  thresholdC
  minStripeLength
  maxStripeGap
  lineSmoothing
  anomalyThreshold
  minSupportingStripes
  minSupportingFrames
  candidateMergeRadius
  maxCameraMotion
}

Persist debug values locally.

Provide "Reset defaults".


---

# 20. DATA MODEL

ScanSession:

- id
- createdAt
- panelType
- panelLabel
- deviceModel
- cameraResolution
- patternMode
- patternConfiguration
- scanDuration
- qualityScore
- processingVersion
- status
- thumbnailPath

ScanFrameMetadata:

- frameIndex
- timestamp
- patternType
- patternPhase
- brightness
- deviceOrientation
- optional motion values

DentCandidate:

- id
- scanId
- normalizedX
- normalizedY
- estimatedRadius
- severity
- confidence
- supportingFrameCount

Do not store huge raw frame blobs directly in SQLite.
Store file paths.


---

# 21. SCAN HISTORY

Simple local history.

Show:
- thumbnail
- panel name
- date
- dent count
- quality score

Allow:
- open
- delete
- rescan

No cloud sync in MVP.


---

# 22. OPTIONAL REPORT

If easy to implement after the scanner works:

Generate a simple shareable result image or PDF.

But this is NOT a core technical milestone.

Do not spend significant time on PDF generation before the scanner works.


---

# 23. SETTINGS

MVP settings:

- default brightness
- default pattern mode
- save raw videos: on/off
- keep debug frames: on/off
- processing quality:
  - fast
  - balanced
  - high quality

Developer settings:
- CV debug mode
- threshold controls
- show FPS
- show processing time
- show device/camera information


---

# 24. PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS

Scanner must not freeze UI.

Never run expensive CV work on the main UI isolate/thread.

Use:
- Dart isolates where appropriate
- native background processing
- frame sampling

Target:
- UI remains responsive at all times
- pattern animation stays smooth
- capture timestamps remain consistent

Processing can occur:
- during scan partially
- after scan fully

For MVP prefer:
record first → process after scan

This makes debugging easier and reduces dropped frames.


---

# 25. IMPORTANT TECHNICAL RISK

The application simultaneously uses:
- front camera
- screen as strong light source

This can create:
- screen flicker artifacts
- rolling shutter artifacts
- exposure instability
- reflections from ambient environment
- screen brightness limitations
- autofocus instability
- PWM brightness effects

Therefore build diagnostics.

Record:
- device FPS
- frame timestamps
- pattern timing
- brightness
- device model

We need to learn which phones work best.


---

# 26. DEVICE TEST MATRIX

Initially test on at least:

- one recent iPhone
- one recent Samsung Galaxy
- one recent Google Pixel if available

For every device record:

- front camera resolution
- FPS
- autofocus behavior
- exposure behavior
- screen brightness
- detected stripe quality
- dent visibility
- thermal behavior

Do not assume behavior is identical across devices.


---

# 27. EXPERIMENTAL VALIDATION

Before adding AI, validate the physical signal.

Create a test dataset.

For each dent:

- photograph panel normally
- record scan with wide stripes
- record scan with medium stripes
- record scan with fine stripes
- record scan with moving stripes
- record distance
- record approximate dent diameter
- record panel type
- record lighting conditions

Initial target dataset:
50–100 real dents.

Include:
- shallow hail dents
- medium dents
- larger parking dents
- false-positive areas
- body lines
- handles
- panel edges
- dirt/reflections


---

# 28. MVP SUCCESS CRITERIA

The MVP is successful if:

1. Structured stripes from the phone screen are clearly visible in the front-camera recording through reflection on the car panel.

2. The CV pipeline can consistently extract stripe geometry.

3. Visible dents produce measurable local stripe distortion.

4. The same dent can be tracked across multiple frames.

5. The system can create a basic dent map.

6. The technician can complete:
   new scan → scan → processing → result

without external hardware.

A desirable early benchmark:
- reliable detection of obvious dents approximately 8–10 mm and larger in controlled indoor conditions.

This is a target, not a guaranteed specification.


---

# 29. DO NOT BUILD YET

Do NOT implement the following before the core optical/CV hypothesis works:

- CRM
- user accounts
- Stripe payments
- subscriptions
- cloud backend
- multi-tenant system
- customer management
- VIN decoding
- insurance integrations
- automatic repair pricing
- marketplace
- technician scheduling
- advanced PDF reports
- social login
- admin dashboard
- external hardware integration
- full 3D reconstruction
- exact dent depth measurement
- AR navigation
- custom AI model training

Keep the product focused.


---

# 30. PHASE 1 DEVELOPMENT ORDER

Implement in this exact order:

1. Flutter project setup
2. Navigation and base architecture
3. Structured-light pattern renderer
4. Light Test screen
5. Front camera capture
6. Simultaneous pattern display + camera recording
7. Save scan locally
8. CV Debug Lab
9. Stripe detection
10. Stripe centerline extraction
11. Frame stabilization
12. Local distortion detection
13. Temporal aggregation
14. Dent candidate map
15. Result screen
16. Scan history
17. Scan quality score

Do not jump to later features before earlier stages are working.


---

# 31. CODING RULES

General:

- Write production-quality, readable code.
- Avoid giant files.
- Avoid giant widgets.
- Avoid magic numbers.
- Use typed models.
- Use dependency injection via Riverpod.
- Keep CV code independent from Flutter widgets.
- Keep platform/native code behind interfaces.
- Add comments only where logic is non-obvious.
- Prefer composition over inheritance.
- Use immutable state where practical.
- Use async APIs correctly.
- Never block UI thread.

Naming:
- clear descriptive English names
- no abbreviations unless standard

Error handling:
- camera permission denied
- camera unavailable
- front camera unavailable
- insufficient storage
- failed recording
- unsupported resolution
- processing failure

Every user-facing error must have a clear recovery action.


---

# 32. TESTING RULES

Write tests for:

- pattern configuration
- scan session state transitions
- dent candidate merging
- confidence calculation
- scan quality calculation
- persistence

For CV:
use saved fixture images/video frames.

Create:
test/fixtures/cv/

Include deterministic test frames when available.

Do not rely only on live camera testing.


---

# 33. LOGGING

Implement structured debug logging.

Categories:

- CAMERA
- LIGHT
- SCAN
- CV
- STORAGE
- PERFORMANCE

Log:
- scan start/end
- FPS
- dropped frames if known
- processing duration
- stripe count
- candidate count
- quality score
- errors

Do not log sensitive user content unnecessarily.


---

# 34. UX RULES

This application is a professional tool for technicians.

UI must be:
- fast
- minimal
- high contrast
- usable in a workshop
- usable with one hand where possible

Prefer:
- dark UI during scan
- large buttons
- simple language
- minimal modal dialogs

During actual scanning:
the display pattern must occupy as much of the screen as possible.


---

# 35. FUTURE ARCHITECTURE — DO NOT IMPLEMENT YET

Architecture should leave room for:

### Advanced light patterns
- phase-shifting structured light
- Gray-code patterns
- sinusoidal patterns
- adaptive stripe density

### AI
Possible later model:
- classifier for candidate dents
- false-positive rejection
- dent severity classification
- segmentation model

Possible stacks:
- TensorFlow Lite
- Core ML
- ONNX Runtime Mobile

### Optional hardware
Future versions may support:
- external LED panel
- phone clip
- fixed phone-to-light geometry
- Bluetooth-controlled light
- calibrated accessory

But current MVP must work without hardware.

### Business features
Later:
- estimates
- before/after comparison
- reports
- customer approval
- hail counting
- repair pricing
- shop/team accounts
- subscription


---

# 36. IMPORTANT PRODUCT LANGUAGE

Internally:
- dent candidate
- anomaly
- stripe distortion
- confidence
- scan quality

Avoid claiming:
- exact 3D measurement
- exact depth
- guaranteed dent detection
- insurance-grade measurement

until validated.


---

# 37. FIRST DEVELOPMENT MILESTONE

The FIRST real milestone is not a polished app.

It is:

> Display structured stripes on the phone while simultaneously recording the reflected stripes from the car panel using the front-facing camera, save the recording, and provide a debug screen where stripe deformation can be visually inspected frame by frame.

Nothing should take priority over this milestone.

If this does not work physically, stop and reassess the optical setup before building more features.


---

# 38. SECOND DEVELOPMENT MILESTONE

After milestone 1 works:

> Automatically extract reflected stripe centerlines and visualize them over the recorded frames.

Display:
- original frame
- detected stripes
- fitted centerlines

No dent detection is required yet.


---

# 39. THIRD DEVELOPMENT MILESTONE

After stripe detection works:

> Detect localized curvature anomalies and aggregate them across frames into stable dent candidates.

Then render:
- circle / marker
- confidence
- candidate count


---

# 40. DEFINITION OF MVP COMPLETE

MVP is complete when a technician can:

1. Open the app
2. Tap New Scan
3. Select a panel
4. Use Auto or manual light pattern
5. Scan a real car panel using only the smartphone
6. Stop scan
7. Process captured frames
8. See automatically detected dent candidates
9. Review scan quality
10. Save scan locally
11. Open the scan later from history

No account or internet connection should be required.


---

# 41. CURSOR EXECUTION INSTRUCTIONS

When generating code:

1. Do not invent backend services.
2. Do not add authentication.
3. Do not change the selected stack without a concrete technical reason.
4. Prefer incremental implementation.
5. Before major refactors, preserve currently working scan functionality.
6. Keep all experimental CV parameters configurable.
7. Always expose intermediate CV output in Debug Lab.
8. Avoid fake placeholder AI.
9. Never label random heuristic output as "AI".
10. Optimize for physical validation first, product polish second.

When asked to implement a feature:
- inspect existing architecture
- reuse existing abstractions
- avoid duplicate services
- keep scanner pipeline modular
- add tests where reasonable

If a technical limitation is discovered:
- document it clearly
- propose the smallest viable workaround
- do not silently change product behavior.


